\description{
This function grafts missing taxa onto an ultrametric, time-calibrated backbone phylogeny as clades sister to clades of the backbone.
Missing taxa sharing the same sister clade are grafted together as ONE missing clade, on the stem branch of their sister clade.

The divergence time of the missing clade is drawn from a birth-death model fitted on the sister clade,
and the missing clade is grown under the same model, conditioned on its number of taxa and its stem age.
}
\details{
\subsection{Stop conditions}{

The function stops, before any grafting, if the backbone is not ultrametric, or if a sister clade is the whole tree (it has no stem branch to graft on).
}

\subsection{Grafting process}{

Groups of missing taxa sharing the same sister clade are ordered by the size of the sister clade, so nested sister clades are processed first.
For each group:
\enumerate{
\item A birth-death model is fitted with \code{phytools::fit.bd()} on the sister clade, or on the smallest enclosing clade with at least \code{min_tips} tips.
The model is fitted on the crown group of this clade, with the sampling fraction of this crown group (see Sampling fraction and unsampled species).
\item The divergence time of the missing clade is drawn on the stem branch of the sister clade with \code{TreeSim::corsim()}:
between the crown age of the sister clade and its stem age (or between the present and the stem age for a single-tip sister clade).
\item A single missing taxon is grafted as a new tip at this divergence time. For several taxa, a missing clade is grown with \code{TreeSim::sim.bd.taxa.age()}
under the fitted model, conditioned on the number of taxa and on its stem age (the drawn divergence time), and grafted on the stem branch of the sister clade.
}
}

\subsection{Enclosing clades}{

When a sister clade contains fewer tips than \code{min_tips}, an alternative higher clade is selected to fit the birth-death model.
This 'enclosing clade' is identified by walking rootward the tree node by node, starting from the sister clade, until a clade meets the \code{min_tips} condition.
This ensures that the diversification dynamics modeled are based on enough tips to be relevant, while being closely associated with the sister clade.
}

\subsection{Sampling fraction}{

The birth-death model is fitted on the crown group of the sister clade or its enclosing clade, so the sampling fraction is:
\code{n_present / (n_present + n_pending + n_unsampled)}, with \code{n_present} the tips of the fitted clade in the current tree
(backbone tips and taxa already grafted, so missing clades grafted in nested sister clades processed earlier are not missing anymore),
\code{n_pending} the taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list} still to graft whose sister clade is strictly nested within the fitted clade,
and \code{n_unsampled} the unsampled species derived from \code{total_richness_per_clades} (0 without it).

Taxa that are sister to the fitted clade itself are outside of its crown group, so they are not counted:
when the sister clade itself is fitted (i.e. have at least \code{min_tips} tips) and nothing is pending nor unsampled within it, the sampling fraction is 1.
When an enclosing clade is fitted, the taxa to graft on the sister clade (nested in the enclosing clade) are counted.
The divergence time of each missing clade is still drawn with \code{TreeSim::corsim(missing = 1)}: unsampled species only influence the fitted rates,
they do not take a branch.
}

\subsection{Unsampled species}{

For a focal clade with a \code{total_richness} value provided: \code{nb_unsampled = total_richness - nb_backbone - nb_missing}, with \code{nb_missing} the number of taxa to graft
as missing clades within the focal clade (because their sister clade is nested within the focal clade), therefore to be included in this focal clade during grafting.
For a clade with no \code{total_richness} value (\code{NA}, or enclosing a sister clade without being listed), \code{nb_unsampled} is the sum of \code{nb_unsampled} over the largest clades nested within it with a \code{total_richness} value provided.
If there is none, \code{nb_unsampled = 0}. The rationale is that even if \code{nb_unsampled} cannot be derived from its \code{total_richness}
(as it is not provided for this clade), it has to at least account for the \code{nb_unsampled} species that have being identified in its own nested clades.

If \code{total_richness_per_clades = NULL}, \code{nb_unsampled = 0}: the clades have no unsampled species (the same as providing only \code{NA} in \verb{$total_richness}).

Each missing clade is grafted on the stem branch of its sister clade in all the grafted phylogenies: only the divergence times vary among simulations and maps.
The species present in each listed sister clade, and the resulting sampling fractions for downstream analyses, are therefore the same in all the grafted phylogenies.
Use \code{\link[=extract_sampling_info]{phylograftR::extract_sampling_info()}} to extract them a posteriori.

The function stops, before any grafting, if \code{total_richness_per_clades} does not cover all the sister clades,
if a \code{total_richness} provided is lower than the number of backbone species plus missing species of the clade,
or if a clade has fewer unsampled species than the sum of the clades with an informed \code{total_richness} nested within it.
}

\subsection{Species attributes: allowed states of a simmap}{

A \code{simmap} describes the states of a discrete attribute (e.g., a trait or a range) along the branches of the backbone: each branch is cut in pieces, each piece being in one state.
Missing clades are grafted on the stem branch of a clade of the backbone, and the function assumes that the user considers this stem branch a valid place for them.
Restricting the grafting to some states of the stem branch is therefore only useful when the user has a strong hypothesis about a change of state
along the stem branch that should affect where the missing clade can be grafted. For most uses, the \code{simmap} is not needed.

For example, in biogeography: a stem fossil is found in range A, and the sister clade is in range A, while the missing clade is in range B.
If the shift from range B to range A on the stem branch of the sister clade is estimated to have occurred before the age of the fossil,
the missing clade can be restricted to the part of the stem branch before the shift, with \code{allowed_states = "B"}.

With a \code{simmap}, a missing clade is only grafted on the pieces of the stem branch of its sister clade that are in its allowed states
(\code{missing_taxa_list$allowed_states} of its taxa if provided, otherwise the global argument \code{allowed_states}).
The divergence time of the missing clade is drawn with \code{TreeSim::corsim()} as without a \code{simmap}: if it falls in a part of the stem branch that is not in an allowed state,
it is drawn again, up to \code{max_age_redraws} times.

The \code{simmap} can also be provided so that the states are updated alongside the phylogeny: the whole missing clade (all its taxa and branches)
inherits the state of the point of the stem branch where it is grafted. The updated \code{simmap} is returned in \verb{$simmap}, if \code{return_simmap = TRUE}).
The allowed states are not required: without any allowed states, all the states are allowed, grafting is not restricted by the states,
but the simmap will be updated, assuming grafted clades inherit the state of the point where they are grafted.

The function stops, before any grafting, if the taxa of a same missing clade have different allowed states, or if the stem branch of a sister clade has no piece in the allowed states
(the clades are listed), and warns about the missing clades with a small share of their stem branch in allowed states (see \code{min_valid_fraction}).

\strong{Cautions for interpretation: risks of circularity.}

Constraining the grafting by a trait that will then be analyzed (e.g., the association of the trait with diversification) may bias the results towards the constraint.
The grafted taxa are placed only where the trait allows it, so they are in the allowed states by construction. This can create or inflate an association between the trait and the diversification dynamics,
affect the rates of transitions between states, or inflate the phylogenetic signal of the trait. It also reduces the uncertainty about the state of the grafted clades since they always inherit a unique state.
It is recommended to acknowledge these potential biases when interpreting such analyses.

To limit the risk of circularity, multiple approaches are possible:
\itemize{
\item constrain the grafting by traits that are not the object of downstream analysis;
\item compare the results with and without the constraint (or on the backbone only);
\item keep the constraint as loose as the information allows (e.g., allowed states that include neighboring states);
\item use several maps and simulations to propagate the uncertainty in ancestral state estimates.
}
}

\subsection{Biogeographic ranges as states}{

With \code{treat_states_as_ranges = TRUE}, the states of the \code{simmap} are biogeographic ranges, coded as in \code{BioGeoBEARS}
(one capital letter per area, and the letters of a multi-area range in alphabetical order, e.g., \code{"AB"}),
and the allowed states are the ranges a missing clade can have.

An allowed range matches a state of the \code{simmap} if it is contained in it: a missing clade allowed in \code{"A"} can be grafted on the parts of the stem branch in \code{"A"}, \code{"AB"} or \code{"ABC"},
whereas a missing clade allowed in \code{"AB"} only can be grafted on the parts of the stem branch in \code{"AB"} or \code{"ABC"}.

When multiple allowed states are provided, the inherited range is drawn randomly from the allowed ranges enclosed within the range of the stem branch piece where it is grafted.
When grafting on an edge with a larger range than the inherited range (e.g., \code{"A"} on \code{"AB"}), this results in a subset sympatric speciation event such as (AB) -> (AB), (A).
In the returned \code{simmap}, the branches of the grafted clades are in their inherited range, whereas the stem branch it is grafted on continues in its own range.
}

\subsection{Multiple simulations}{

With \code{nb_simulations > 1}, the grafting is simulated \code{nb_simulations} times from the same backbone and the same \code{missing_taxa_list}.
Each simulation starts again from the backbone: the birth-death models are fitted on the tree of the simulation (they depend on the taxa already grafted),
and the simulations do not share anything but the inputs. The phylogenies are returned in a \code{"multiPhylo"} object (a single \code{"phylo"} if \code{nb_simulations = 1}),
and the memory used grows with the number of simulations.

The first simulation uses \code{seed}, so it is the same phylogeny as a run with \code{nb_simulations = 1} and the same \code{seed}.
The seeds of the other simulations are drawn from a stream started by \code{seed}, and returned in the attribute \code{"seeds"}:
a simulation can be reproduced alone with its seed, and the first simulations of a longer run are the same as the ones of a shorter run.
With \code{seed = NULL}, the simulations follow the random number generator of the session.

A failure that is specific to a simulation, because of the random draws (no usable divergence age or time after \code{max_age_redraws} redraws), skips this simulation with a warning:
the name of a phylogeny keeps the index of its simulation (e.g., \code{"sim_3"} is missing if the third simulation failed). The function reports the number of phylogenies obtained
if it differs from \code{nb_simulations}, and stops if no simulation succeeded.
With \code{force_simulations = TRUE}, new seeds are tried until \code{nb_simulations} phylogenies are obtained (up to \code{10 * nb_simulations} attempts):
the phylogenies are then named \code{"sim_1"} to \code{"sim_N"} without gaps, and the seeds of the failed attempts are in the attribute \code{"failed"}.
Any other error (invalid inputs, no branch available for a taxon, ...) concerns all the simulations and stops the whole run.

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each simulation saves its own temporary file (see 'Temporary saves and resuming').
}

\subsection{Multiple stochastic maps}{

When multiple stochastic maps of the same trait are provided as a \code{"multiSimmap"}, the grafting is run with each of its maps.
The maps are alternative hypotheses of the evolution of the same trait (or range), used as alternative constraints.
All the maps and the simulations are crossed: each map is used for \code{nb_simulations} simulations, so \code{P} maps give \code{P * nb_simulations} grafted phylogenies.
This can lead to a quick inflation of computation time, thus a warning is sent if the total number of grafted phylogenies (\code{P * nb_simulations}) is more than 1000.

The output is a list with one element per stochastic map, named after the maps (the names of the \code{"multiSimmap"} if it has some, otherwise \code{"map_1"}, ..., \code{"map_P"}).
With \code{nb_simulations = 1}, each element is a grafted phylogeny. With several simulations, each element is a \code{"multiPhylo"} (see 'Multiple simulations')
with phylogenies named \code{"<map>_sim_1"}, \code{"<map>_sim_2"}, ... and the attributes \code{"seeds"} and \code{"failed"} of the map.
Each phylogeny can be reproduced alone with its map (as a single \code{simmap}) and its seed.

A map for which no simulation could be obtained is dropped from the output with a warning, and listed in the attribute \code{"failed_maps"} of the list
(data.frame with the columns \code{map} and \code{reason}). The function stops if no map gives any phylogeny.

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each map and each simulation saves its own temporary file (see 'Temporary saves and resuming').
}

\subsection{Several traits}{

The maps of several traits can be used at once to constrain grafting. \code{simmap} is then a list with one element per trait, each a \code{"simmap"} or a \code{"multiSimmap"}
(e.g., \code{simmap = list(color = color_simmap, elevation = elevation_simmap)}). The elements can be named after the traits (with different names that cannot contain \verb{;}, \verb{,}, \code{=} or \code{|}),
otherwise the traits are named \code{"trait_1"}, \code{"trait_2"}, ... All the maps must have the same tips, topology and branch lengths as the backbone \code{phy}.

A taxon can only be grafted on the parts of the branches that are in one of its allowed states, for every trait.
The allowed states are provided for each trait, in the order of the list of maps (or named after the traits):
the global argument \code{allowed_states} is a list with one element per trait, e.g., \code{list(c("blue", "green"), NULL, "high")} for three traits,
and \code{missing_taxa_list$allowed_states} has such a list for each taxon.
A trait with \code{NULL} (or an empty vector, or \code{NA}) has no taxon-level value, so the global \code{allowed_states} of the trait is used for the taxon
(a taxon-level value replaces the global one, trait by trait, as with a single trait).
A trait with no states, neither at the taxon level nor globally, has all its states allowed (no restriction).
Other traits can still be restricted: \code{list(NULL, "high")} only restricts the second trait.

With \code{return_simmap = TRUE}, \verb{$simmap} is a list of updated simmaps, one per trait, named after the traits.
In each simmap, a grafted branch is entirely in the state of each trait at the point where it was grafted (each trait is inherited from the lineage it was grafted on).

With a list of \code{"multiSimmap"} provided as \code{simmap}, the maps of the traits are paired: the grafting is run with the map 1 of every trait, then the map 2 of every trait, and so on.
All the traits must therefore have the same number of maps to be associated in a given grafting process.

One of the traits can be treated as biogeographic ranges combined with any number of other traits.
Use a logical vector in \code{treat_states_as_ranges}to define which of the trait is to be treated as biogeographic ranges for inheritance.
The range logic is the one of 'Biogeographic ranges as states' for the range trait:
its allowed values for grafting are any ranges that encompass the allowed range.
For instance, a missing clade allowed in \code{"A;high"} can be grafted on stem branch pieces in \code{"A;high"}, \code{"AB;high"} or \code{"ABC;high"},
whereas a missing clade allowed in \code{"AB;high"} only can be grafted on stem branch pieces in \code{"AB;high"} or \code{"ABC;high"}.

The other traits are matched exactly, and are inherited as they are from the point where the clade is grafted: with \code{"AB"} and \code{"high"} at the graft point and the allowed range \code{"A"},
the whole missing clade inherits \code{"A"} and \code{"high"}. Only one trait can be treated as ranges to prevent inflation of the number of combined allowed states to consider.
}

\subsection{Parallel computation}{

The simulations based on the different stochastic maps and seeds are independent, so they can be run in parallel with the package \code{future} and \code{future.apply}.
Both packages must be installed independently from phylograftR (they are only suggested).

The grafting function does not manage the workers: you must set a plan beforehand, e.g., \code{future::plan(future::multisession, workers = 4)},
and reset it afterwards with \code{future::plan(future::sequential)}. With the default \code{parallel = NULL}, the function uses the plan if there is one, \code{parallel = FALSE} ignores it.
All the \code{P * nb_simulations} simulations (with \code{P} stochastic maps per trait) are run as one set of tasks, so the workers are used evenly.

The output is exactly the same as without parallelization.
The messages, warnings and printed output of the simulations are relayed to the session when the tasks are collected (so they appear after the simulations, not live),
and with \code{verbose = TRUE} one line is printed when each simulation is done.

Setting up the workers and sending the data to them has a time cost, so parallelization only pays off with large backbone phylogenies, or many simulations or maps.
As a rule of thumb, use parallel computation when the sequential run would last more than a minute or so, i.e., for large backbone phylogenies (500 tips or more) and several grafted phylogenies
(\code{nb_maps * nb_simulations}), or for 50 or more grafted phylogenies on small backbones.
}

\subsection{Temporary saves and resuming}{

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each grafted phylogeny (one per simulation and per stochastic map) is saved in its own \code{.rds} file, built from \code{temp_file_path} by adding
the suffixes \code{"_map_<name>"} when a \code{"multiSimmap"} is used, then \code{"_sim_<k>"} when \code{nb_simulations > 1}.
For instance, with \code{temp_file_path = "dir/temp.rds"}:
\itemize{
\item a single phylogeny uses \code{"dir/temp.rds"};
\item several simulations use \code{"dir/temp_sim_1.rds"}, \code{"dir/temp_sim_2.rds"}, ...;
\item several maps use \code{"dir/temp_map_A.rds"}, \code{"dir/temp_map_B.rds"}, ...;
\item several simulations across several maps use both suffixes as \code{"dir/temp_map_A_sim_1.rds"}.
}

With \code{force_simulations = TRUE}, \verb{<k>} is the number of the attempt, not necessarily the ID to which the grafted phylogeny will be attributed to in the final output.
This is required to identify the appropriate seed used to produce each attempt; attempts that failed leave a file too for tractability.

Temporary files can be read with \code{readRDS()} and used to plot the grafted phylogeny in its current state.
The object is a regular \code{"phylo"} with the attribute \code{"phylograftR_temp"} that stores what is needed to resume a run.
Once the phylogeny is fully grafted, the file is replaced by the complete output of the simulation (with \verb{$simmap}, \verb{$grafted_tips}, etc.).

With \code{clean_temp_files = TRUE} (default), all the temporary files of the run are deleted once the function returns without crashing.
A run that crashes or is interrupted never deletes its files, so it can be resumed with \code{resume = TRUE}.
Use \code{clean_temp_files = FALSE} to keep the files after a successful run.

With \code{resume = TRUE}, the function must be called again with \strong{exactly the same arguments} as the interrupted run.
For each phylogeny, its temporary file is searched: a complete phylogeny is reused as it is, an unfinished one is continued from its last save, and a phylogeny without file is grafted from scratch.

Resuming is not equivalent to grafting the taxa left on the temporary phylogeny used as a new backbone, with a \code{missing_taxa_list} restricted to these taxa:
the taxa already grafted would be treated as backbone species, so the sampling fractions of the birth-death models, the stem of the target clades,
the identification of the grafted tips and the updated \code{simmap} would be wrong, and a \code{simmap} of the initial backbone would not match the temporary phylogeny.
Use \code{resume = TRUE} instead.
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Create a toy backbone phylogeny: ultrametric, with a root age of 50 time units
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

# List the missing taxa to graft
missing_clades_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2", "M3", "M4"),
  # Sister_clade is named after Taxon_name (same names, same order)
  # M1 and M2 share the same sister clade: they are grafted together as one missing clade
  Sister_clade = list(M1 = c("t8", "t22"), M2 = c("t8", "t22"), M3 = c("t26", "t18"), M4 = "t5"))

# --- Perform grafting --- #

# Graft the missing clades
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_clades(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                    verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
plot(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)

# Highlight the grafted tips and branches in red
plot_grafted(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)


# --- Produce several grafted phylogenies --- #

# The grafting is stochastic: several simulations can be run at once from the same backbone and list
# (a "multiPhylo" named sim_1, sim_2, ...; sim_1 is the same as a single run with the same seed)
grafted_phys <- graft_missing_clades(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                      nb_simulations = 3, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
names(grafted_phys)
# Seeds of the simulations, to reproduce each phylogeny alone
attr(grafted_phys, "seeds")

# Plot phylos
old_par <- par()["mfrow"]
par(mfrow = c(1, 3))
plot_grafted(grafted_phys, index = 1, cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #1") # sim_1
plot_grafted(grafted_phys, index = 2, cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #2") # sim_2
plot_grafted(grafted_phys, index = 3, cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #3") # sim_3
par(old_par)


# --- Account for unsampled species --- #

# Account for unsampled species with the total richness of the sister clades.
# All the unique sister clades must be listed (NA = unknown richness)
total_richness_per_clades <- list(
  Sister_clade = list(c("t8", "t22"), c("t26", "t18"), "t5"),
  total_richness = c(20, NA, 3))
grafted_phy_richness <- graft_missing_clades(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                             total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades,
                                             verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Inspect the resulting clade richness and sampling fractions
extract_sampling_info(phy_grafted = grafted_phy_richness,
                      total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades)

# --- Apply attribute-based exclusions --- #

# Restrict the grafting to some states of a stochastic character map (simmap) of the backbone
# The toy simmap has three states, "blue", "green" and "red"
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_simmap)

# List the missing taxa to graft
missing_clades_list <- list(
   Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2", "M3", "M4", "M5"),
   Sister_clade = list(M1 = c("t26", "t18"), M2 = c("t26", "t18"), M3 = c("t26", "t18"),
                       M4 = "t14", M5 = "t14"))

# The simmap shows that the targeted stem branches recorded state shifts.
plot(toy_simmap, col = c("blue" = "dodgerblue", "green" = "limegreen", "red" = "firebrick"))

# We can use 'allowed_states' to limit grafting on the "green" portions of the stem branches
   # The stem branch from the "bottom" clade shifts from "green" to "red"
   # The terminal (stem) branch of "t14" shift from "green" to "red" too
grafted_phy_states <- graft_missing_clades(phy = toy_backbone,
                                           missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                           simmap = toy_simmap, allowed_states = "green",
                                           verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Plot output phylogeny with colored grafts
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_states, use_simmap = FALSE, cex = 0.7)

# The updated simmap is in the output: the new grafted clades branch from the "green" segments
# of the stem branches of their sister clades, and each newly grafted clades are entirely "green"
# Grafted branches are dashed lines, and grafted tips are labelled in red.
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_states,
             simmap_colors = c("blue" = "dodgerblue", "green" = "limegreen", "red" = "firebrick"))


# --- Use several stochastic maps (alternative hypotheses of trait evolution) as constraints --- #

# A 'multiSimmap' gives several stochastic maps of the same trait:
# the grafting is run with each of them.
# Here, 3 maps are simulated from the states of the tips of the toy simmap.
tip_states <- phytools::getStates(toy_simmap, type = "tips")

# (the random number generator of the session is restored afterwards)
had_seed <- exists(".Random.seed", envir = globalenv(), inherits = FALSE)
if (had_seed) old_seed <- get(".Random.seed", envir = globalenv(), inherits = FALSE)
set.seed(118)
toy_multiSimmap <- phytools::make.simmap(toy_backbone, tip_states,
                                         model = "ER", nsim = 3, message = FALSE)
if (had_seed)
{ assign(".Random.seed", old_seed, envir = globalenv())
} else { rm(".Random.seed", envir = globalenv()) }

# Graft using constraints from all 3 simmaps X 2 simulations per simmap
grafted_phys_maps <- graft_missing_clades(phy = toy_backbone,
                                         missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                         simmap = toy_multiSimmap, allowed_states = "green",
                                         nb_simulations = 2, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Inspect output:
# A list with one element per simmap, each a multiPhylo with the simulations of the map
names(grafted_phys_maps)
names(grafted_phys_maps$map_2)
# The map and the seed of a phylogeny are included as attributes for reproducibility
attr(grafted_phys_maps$map_2[[1]], "map")
attr(grafted_phys_maps$map_2[[1]], "seed")


# --- Constraints from several traits at once --- #

# Load the simmap for the second trait: 'toy_simmap_elevation' has two states: "high" and "low".
data(toy_simmap_elevation)

# Provide the maps of the traits as a list
# The global 'allowed_states' has one element per trait (here, "green" color and "low" elevation).
grafted_phy_traits <- graft_missing_clades(phy = toy_backbone,
                                           missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                           simmap = list(color = toy_simmap,
                                                         elevation = toy_simmap_elevation),
                                           allowed_states = list("green", "low"),
                                           verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# '$simmap' is a list of updated simmaps, one per trait
names(grafted_phy_traits$simmap)

# Newly grafted clades can only be grafted on and harbor the allowed state in each trait
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_traits, trait = 1) # Only 'green'
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_traits, trait = 2) # Only 'low'


# --- Constraints from biogeographic ranges and another trait at once --- #

# One of the traits can be treated as biogeographic ranges:
# the allowed ranges do not have to match exactly the range of target stem branch pieces,
# any allowed range comprised in a target range is valid (e.g., "A" can be grafted on "AB").
# Here, the first trait holds ranges.

# Load the range simmap
data(toy_simmap_ranges)

# The clades are grafted on stem branch pieces in ranges
# containing "A" or "B" and in elevation "low"
grafted_phy_ranges_traits <- graft_missing_clades(phy = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
    simmap = list(biogeo = toy_simmap_ranges, elevation = toy_simmap_elevation),
    allowed_states = list(c("A", "B"), "low"),
    treat_states_as_ranges = c(biogeo = TRUE, elevation = FALSE),
    verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# '$simmap' is a list of updated simmaps, one per trait: ranges, and elevation
names(grafted_phy_ranges_traits$simmap)

# Newly grafted clades can only harbor the allowed state/range in each trait
# They can be grafted on range that encompasses their allowed range (here, "AB")

# Range: Grafted on "A", "B", or "AB", but inherit only "A" or "B".
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_traits, trait = 1)
# Elevation: Only 'low'
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_traits, trait = 2)


}
